readings.c1s1:
¶
'''unbuilt''' (un-bilt′), ''a.'' Not yet built; not erected; unconstructed.
{{smaller block|''Unbuilt'' Babel.
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, iv.}}}}

'''unbundle''' (un-bun′dl), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''bundle.''] To unpack; open; disclose; declare. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|''Unbundle'' your griefs, madam, and let us into the particulars.
{{right|''Jarvis'', Don Quixote, II. iii. 6. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''unbuoyed''' (un-boid′ or un-böid′), ''a.'' Not buoyed or borne up. ''Edinburgh Rev.''

'''unburden, unburthen''' (un-bėr′dn, -ᴛ̵ʜn), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''burden''<sup>1</sup>, ''burthen''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' To rid of a load; free from a burden; ease.
{{smaller block|<poem>While we
''Unburthen’d'' crawl toward death.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 1. 42.}}}}
'''2.''' To throw off as being a burden; discharge; hence, to disclose; reveal.
{{smaller block|To ''unburden'' all my plots and purposes.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., i. 1. 133.}}}}
'''3.''' To relieve, as the mind or heart, by disclosing what lies heavy on it; also, reflexively, to relieve (one’s self) in this way: as, he ''unburdened himself'' to his confessor.
{{smaller block|Well, now we are alone, there is a subject, my dear friend, on which I wish to ''unburthen'' my mind to you.
{{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, iv. 3.}}
Georgina, when not ''unburdening'' her heart to me, spent most of her time in lying on the sofa, fretting about the dullness of the house. ''Charlotte Brontë'', Jane Eyre, xxi.}}

'''unburdened''' (un-bėr′dnd), ''a.'' Not burdened.
{{smaller block|Wholly ''unburdened'' with historical knowledge or with any experience of life. ''The Academy'', Aug. 2, 1890, p. 96.}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
'''uncalled''' (un-kâld′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''uncalled''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''called''.] Not called; unsummoned; not invited; not demanded.
{{smaller block|Mild Lucina came ''uncalled''.
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Ovid’s Cinyras and Myrrha.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Uncalled for''', not required; not needed or demanded; improperly brought forward.}}
{{smaller block|In other people’s presence I was, as formerly, deferential and quiet; any other line of conduct being ''uncalled for''.
{{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Jane Eyre, xxiv.}}}}
{{smaller|Also written ''uncalled-for'', when used attributively: as, most ''uncalled-for'' remarks.}}

'''uncallow''' (un-kal′ō), ''n.'' The name given in Norfolk, England, to the deposits of gravel resting on the chalk.

'''uncalm''' (un-käm′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''calm''.] To deprive of calm; disturb. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|What strange disquiet has ''uncalm’d'' your breast.
{{right|''Dryden''.}}}}

'''uncamp''' (un-kamp′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''camp''<sup>1</sup>.] To cause to decamp; dislodge; expel. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|If they could but now ''uncamp'' their enemies.
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., ii.}}}}

'''uncandid''' (un-kan′did), ''a.'' Not candid, frank, or true. ''The American'', VIII. 232.

'''uncandidly''' (un-kan′did-li), ''adv.'' In an uncandid manner.

'''uncandor''' (un-kan′dọr), ''n.'' Lack of candor. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|“It seems to me it was an utter failure,” suggested Annie. “Quite. But it was what I expected.” There appeared an ''uncandor'' in this which Annie could not let pass.
{{right|''Howells'', Annie Kilburn, xxi.}}}}

'''uncanniness''' (un-kan′i-nes), ''n.'' The character

readings.c2s2:
of being uncanny.
{{smaller block|Your general ''uncanniness.''
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, p. 277.}}}}

¶
'''uncanny''' (un-kan′i), ''a.'' [Sc. and North. Eng. See ''canny.''] '''1.''' Not canny, in any sense.—'''2.''' Eery; weird; mysterious; apparently not of this world; hence, noting one supposed to possess preternatural powers.
{{smaller block|I wish she binna ''uncanny.'' ''Scott'', Guy Mannering, iii.

What does that inexplicable, that ''uncanny'' turn of countenance mean? ''Charlotte Brontë'', Jane Eyre, xxiv.

He . . . rather expected something ''uncanny'' to lay hold of him from behind. ''C. Kingsley'', Alton Locke, xxi.}}
'''3.''' Severe, as a fall or blow.
{{smaller block|An ''uncanny'' coup I gat for my pains.
{{right|''Scott'', Waverley, lxvi.}}}}

'''uncanonic''' (un-ka̤-non′ik), ''a.'' Same as ''uncanonical.''
{{smaller block|This act was ''uncanonic'' and a fault.
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, II. 170.}}}}

'''uncanonical''' (un-ka̤-non′i-kal), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not canonical; not agreeable to the canons.
{{smaller block|If ordinations were ''uncanonical.''
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Episcopacy Asserted, § 32.}}}}
'''2.''' Not conformed or conforming to rule; not determined by rule.—'''3.''' Not belonging to the canon (of Scripture).—'''Uncanonical hours.''' {{smaller|See ''hour.''}}

'''uncanonicalness''' (un-ka̤-non′i-kal-nes), ''n.'' The character of being uncanonical. ''Bp. Lloyd.''

'''uncanonize''' (un-kan′o̤n-īz), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''canonize.''] '''1.''' To deprive of canonical authority.—'''2.''' To reduce from the rank of a canonized saint.

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''uncanonized''' (un-kan′ọn-īzd), ''a.'' Not canonized; not enrolled among the saints.
{{smaller block|The members of it [the Romish communion] boast very much of mighty signs and wonders wrought by some canonized and some ''uncanonized'' saints.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury'', Sermons, II. i.}}}}

'''uncap''' (un-kap′), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''uncapped'', ppr. ''uncapping''. [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''cap''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To remove a cap, as a percussion-cap, from, as a gun or a cartridge, or a protecting cap from, as a lens-tube.

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To remove the cap or hat.
{{smaller block|I felt really like ''uncapping'', with a kind of reverence.
{{right|''H. James, Jr.'', Trans. Sketches, p. 247.}}}}

'''uncapable†''' (un-kā′pa-bl), ''a.'' Incapable.
{{smaller block|<poem>An inhuman wretch,
''Uncapable'' of pity.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., iv. 1. 5.}}
He who came to take away the sins of the world was ''uncapable'' of pollution by sin.
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Contemplations, V. 176.}}}}

'''uncape''' (un-kāp′), ''v.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''cape''<sup>2</sup>.] In ''hawking'', to prepare for flying at game by taking off the cape or hood. {{smaller|Various explanations are given to the word as used by Shakspere, “Merry Wives,” iii. 3. 176: “I warrant we’ll unkennel the fox. Let me stop this way first. So now ''uncape''.” According to Steevens, it means to turn the fox out of the bag; according to Warburton, to dig out the fox when earthed; according to Nares, to throw off the dogs or to begin the hunt; according to Schmidt, to uncouple hounds.}}

'''uncaptious''' (un-kap′shus), ''a.'' Not captious; not ready to take objection or offense.

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|''Uncaptious'' and candid natures.
{{right|''Feltham'', Resolves, ii. 43.}}}}

'''uncardinal''' (un-kär′di-nal), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''cardinal.''] To divest of the cardinalate. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Borgio . . . got a dispensation to ''uncardinal'' himself.
{{right|''Fuller.''}}}}

'''uncared''' (un-kãrd′), ''a.'' Not regarded; not heeded; not attended: with ''for.''
{{smaller block|Their own . . . ghostly condition ''uncared for.''
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, v. § 1.}}}}

'''uncareful''' (un-kãr′fu̇l), ''a.'' '''1.''' Having no care; free from care.
{{smaller block|This journey . . . has been one of the brightest and most ''uncareful'' interludes of my life.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', French and Italian Note-Books, p. 272.}}}}
'''2.''' Taking no care; not watchful; incautious. —'''3†.''' Producing no care.

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|''Uncareful'' treasure.{{right|''Quarles.''}}}}

[Rare in all senses.]

'''Uncaria''' (ung-kā′ri-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL. (Schreber, 1789), < L. ''uncus'', a hook: see ''unce''<sup>2</sup>, ''uncus''.] A genus
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''Uncaria Gambier.''<br>''a'', corolla laid open; ''b'', calyx-tube laid open, showing the style and stigma; ''c'', fruit with persistent calyx.}}}}
of gamopetalous plants, of the order ''Rubiaceæ'' and tribe ''Naucleeæ.'' {{smaller|It is distinguished from the type-genus ''Nauclea'' by its valvate corolla and septicidal capsule. There are about 32 species, mostly natives of India beyond the Ganges, with one in Africa and one in Guiana and Brazil. They are shrubby climbers with opposite short-petioled leaves, and axillary heads of hairy yellowish flowers, followed by large elongated, two-celled, many-seeded capsules. ''U. Gambier'', a native of Malacca, Java, and Sumatra, is the source of one of the most important tanning-materials of commerce, for which see ''gambier''.}}

'''uncarnate''' (un-kär′nāt), ''a.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''carnate.''] Not carnate or fleshly; not incarnate; not made flesh.
{{smaller block|The ''uncarnate'' Father.{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err.}}}}
